  <h1>Clean Wild Turkey Breast Lunch Meat</h1>
  <p class="subtitle">Low-temperature Ninja Foodi Grill method for unprocessed sandwich-style turkey breast</p>

  <div class="working-draft">
    <strong>Status:</strong> Working Draft<br>
    This recipe is intended as a first-pass method. Adjust salt, cook time, and seasoning after the first real-world test batch.
  </div>

  <h2>Description</h2>
  <p>
    A simple, clean, homemade turkey lunch meat made from wild turkey breast, using only controlled salt,
    dry seasoning, gentle heat, and a full chill before slicing. This recipe is designed to avoid factory-injected
    solution, mystery flavoring, binders, phosphates, excess sodium, added water weight, and processed deli-meat
    texture tricks.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is intended for cold sandwich slices, not hot roasted turkey dinner slices.
  </p>

  <div class="section">
    <h2>Yield</h2>
    <p>About 1 to 1¼ lb finished sliced turkey, depending on trimming and moisture loss.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="section">
    <h2>Tools &amp; Equipment</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Ninja Foodi Grill with Bake or Roast setting</li>
      <li>Digital instant-read thermometer</li>
      <li>Cutting board</li>
      <li>Sharp knife</li>
      <li>Measuring spoons</li>
      <li>Parchment paper</li>
      <li>Aluminum foil</li>
      <li>Small tray or plate</li>
      <li>Optional: butcher twine</li>
      <li>Optional: food-safe gloves</li>
      <li>Optional: deli slicer or very sharp slicing knife</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="section">
    <h2>Ingredients</h2>

    <h3>Main Ingredient</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>1½ lb wild turkey breast, trimmed</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Dry Brine / Seasoning</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>¾ tsp kosher salt</li>
      <li>½ tsp black pepper</li>
      <li>½ tsp garlic powder</li>
      <li>½ tsp onion powder</li>
      <li>¼ tsp paprika, optional</li>
      <li>¼ tsp dried thyme or poultry seasoning, optional</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Optional Moisture/Flavor Additions</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>1 tsp olive oil or melted butter, optional</li>
      <li>½ tsp Dijon mustard, optional</li>
      <li>A few drops Worcestershire sauce, optional</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      For the first test cook, keep it simple and avoid heavy sauces so the actual flavor of the wild turkey can be judged.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="section">
    <h2>Time</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Prep Time: 10–15 minutes</li>
      <li>Dry Brine Time: 8–24 hours, refrigerated</li>
      <li>Cook Time: approximately 35–70 minutes, depending on breast thickness and starting temperature</li>
      <li>Chill Time: minimum 4 hours; best overnight</li>
      <li>Total Time: same-day minimum about 5–7 hours; best quality requires overnight dry brine plus overnight chill</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="section">
    <h2>Directions</h2>

    <h3>1. Inspect and trim the turkey</h3>
    <p>Look over the wild turkey breast carefully. Remove:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Shot pellets</li>
      <li>Feather bits</li>
      <li>Bruised or bloodshot meat</li>
      <li>Silver skin</li>
      <li>Hard tendons</li>
      <li>Ragged wound-channel meat</li>
      <li>Any jelly-like or metallic-smelling damaged tissue</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Pat the turkey dry with paper towels.</p>

    <h3>2. Season and dry brine</h3>
    <p>
      Mix the salt, pepper, garlic powder, onion powder, and optional paprika or thyme.
      Coat the turkey evenly on all sides.
    </p>
    <p>
      Place the seasoned turkey on a plate or tray, cover, and refrigerate for 8–24 hours.
      This lets the salt move into the meat instead of sitting only on the surface.
    </p>

    <h3>3. Shape for slicing</h3>
    <p>
      For lunch meat, shape matters. If the turkey breast is uneven, fold the thinner end under
      the thicker section to create a more even roast shape.
    </p>
    <p>Optional: tie it lightly with butcher twine.</p>
    <p>No twine method:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Place the turkey on parchment.</li>
      <li>Roll or fold it into a compact shape.</li>
      <li>Wrap the parchment around it.</li>
      <li>Wrap the parchment bundle in foil.</li>
      <li>Crimp the ends to hold the shape.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Do not make it so tight that juices have nowhere to go, but keep it firm enough to hold a loaf-like shape.
    </p>

    <h3>4. Cook gently in the Ninja Foodi Grill</h3>
    <p>Set the Ninja Foodi Grill to:</p>
    <p><strong>Bake or Roast: 250°F</strong></p>
    <p>Place the wrapped turkey in the grill.</p>
    <p>Cook until the thickest center reaches:</p>
    <p><strong>160–165°F internal temperature</strong></p>
    <p>
      For maximum safety, use 165°F as the final target. USDA lists 165°F as the safe minimum internal temperature
      for poultry.
    </p>
    <p>
      For best texture, especially if the meat is still wrapped and resting, pulling around 160°F and allowing carryover
      heat during the wrapped rest may help reduce dryness. The conservative food-safety target is 165°F.
    </p>

    <h3>5. Rest wrapped</h3>
    <p>
      Remove the turkey from the Ninja Foodi Grill. Leave it wrapped and rest at room temperature for 15–20 minutes.
      This helps the juices settle and lets carryover heat finish evening out the center.
    </p>

    <h3>6. Chill completely</h3>
    <p>
      After resting, place the wrapped turkey in the refrigerator. Chill at least 4 hours. Best result: chill overnight.
    </p>
    <p>
      Do not slice hot if the goal is lunch meat. Hot slicing will leak juice and shred the meat.
    </p>

    <h3>7. Slice</h3>
    <p>
      Unwrap the chilled turkey. Slice thinly across the grain. Use for sandwiches, wraps, salads, crackers, or protein plates.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="section">
    <h2>Storage</h2>
    <p>
      Refrigerate sliced turkey in an airtight container. Best quality: 3–4 days.
      For longer storage, freeze in small portions.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="section">
    <h2>Important Notes</h2>

    <h3>Why this method uses the Ninja Foodi Grill instead of the pressure cooker</h3>
    <p>
      The Ninja Foodi Grill gives a slower, more controlled heat rise. That helps the meat set into a firm,
      sliceable lunch-meat texture.
    </p>
    <p>
      A pressure cooker is better for shredded turkey, soup meat, gravy meat, or turkey salad.
      It can make lean wild turkey breast stringy or overcooked because pressure cooking is aggressive and fast.
    </p>

    <h3>Why wild turkey needs special handling</h3>
    <p>
      Wild turkey breast is leaner than most store-bought turkey breast and does not contain factory-added solution.
      It can dry out quickly if overcooked, so brining and controlled cooking are important.
    </p>

    <h3>Salt control</h3>
    <p>
      This recipe starts at ½ tsp kosher salt per pound. That is intentionally conservative.
    </p>
    <p>
      For a stronger deli-meat flavor next time, increase to ¾ tsp kosher salt per pound.
      For 1½ lb turkey, that would be about 1⅛ tsp kosher salt total.
    </p>
    <p>
      Do not make that change until after the first test batch.
    </p>
  </div>

